The shape, verified:

```jsx
// CREATE the channel
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');   // default for no-provider reads

// PROVIDE at the subtree root
function App() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');
  // THE TRAP: this value literal creates a NEW object every render
  // → every consumer re-renders on EVERY App render, theme change or not
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ theme, setTheme }), [theme]);
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={value}>
      <Page />          {/* no props drilled through here */}
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}

// CONSUME anywhere below — skips any depth
function ThemedButton() {
  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);
  return <button className={theme} onClick={() => setTheme('dark')}>Go dark</button>;
}
```

When Context wins (the interview answer):
- Data is genuinely cross-cutting: theme, authenticated user, locale, feature flags
- The tree is deep and intermediate components would be pure pass-throughs
- Updates are LOW-frequency (theme flips, user logs in/out — not per keystroke)

When Context loses (the honest half):
- High-frequency updates: every `value` change re-renders EVERY consumer, and memoized consumers still can't escape it (context bypasses memo). A per-keystroke search string in context re-renders the app.
- One component needs it: just pass a prop.
- State is complex with many transitions: a state library (Zustand/Redux) with selectors — only components reading THAT slice re-render.
